St Basil on the written and unwritten tr:
The clearest early statement of how the Church holds teaching that is not spelt out in Scripture comes from St Basil the Great.

**Who.** St Basil of Caesarea (about 330–379), bishop of Caesarea in Cappadocia, one of the three great **Cappadocian Fathers** with his friend St Gregory of Nazianzus and his brother St Gregory of Nyssa. His feast is on 1 January. He wrote *On the Holy Spirit* in about 375, to defend the Spirit's full divinity.

**The text** (*On the Holy Spirit*, chapter 27, section 66; tr. Blomfield Jackson, NPNF 2nd series vol. 8, 1895):

> "Of the beliefs and practices whether generally accepted or publicly enjoined which are preserved in the Church some we possess derived from written teaching; others we have received delivered to us in a mystery by the tradition of the apostles; and both of these in relation to true religion have the same force."

**What he gives as examples.** Basil lists practices the whole Church kept that no Scripture text commands in so many words:

- signing believers **with the sign of the cross**;
- **turning to the east** to pray;
- the words of the **epiclesis**, the prayer calling down the Spirit at the Eucharist;
- **blessing the water** of baptism and the oil of chrismation;
- **immersing three times** at baptism;
- the **renunciation of Satan** before baptism.

**His argument.** Basil's opponents objected to a form of the doxology, giving glory to the Father *with* the Son *together with* the Holy Spirit, because it was not word for word in Scripture. Basil answers: the Church keeps many things that are not written down, and **"were we to attempt to reject such customs as have no written authority, on the ground that the importance they possess is small, we should unintentionally injure the Gospel in its very vitals."**

**Even the confession of faith is unwritten in this sense.** In section 67 he asks what written source there is for "the very confession of our faith in Father, Son, and Holy Ghost" as it is made at baptism. The baptismal creed was handed on by mouth before it was written.

**What "in a mystery" means.** *Mystḗrion* (μυστήριον) here means something kept within the worshipping Church and taught to the baptised, guarded from the curious and the uninitiated, as Basil explains with the example of the veiled parts of Moses' tabernacle. It is not secret knowledge for an elite inside the Church: these practices were kept by all the faithful, in all the churches.

**Why it matters.** **Basil shows that, for the Fathers, unwritten apostolic tradition is not a rival to Scripture but its companion, kept by the same Church.** He quotes 2 Thessalonians 2:15 to make the point, and later lessons on worship and the Mysteries rest on the practices he names.
